WEBVTT

00:00.793 --> 00:03.972
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.072 --> 00:07.341
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.375 --> 00:10.911
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:10.945 --> 00:13.914
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:13.948 --> 00:17.417
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:17.452 --> 00:20.721
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:20.755 --> 00:22.289
♪ All the things that make us

00:22.323 --> 00:23.657
♪ Laugh and cry

00:23.691 --> 00:24.898
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:33.901 --> 00:35.902
(Brian and Stewie scream)

00:38.757 --> 00:41.058
Hurry, Brian,
they're gaining on us!

00:43.695 --> 00:45.329
(gunfire)

00:47.466 --> 00:48.899
Damn it,
we're surrounded!

00:54.873 --> 00:56.474
Crap, they got away.

00:56.508 --> 00:58.042
Better call the boss.

00:59.644 --> 01:02.513
Damn, I'm not getting
a smoke signal here.

01:02.547 --> 01:03.514
(plays rim shot)

01:03.548 --> 01:05.049
(rainstick rattles)

01:12.691 --> 01:14.592
Damn it, Stewie, what
the hell did you do?!

01:14.626 --> 01:16.127
I know, I know,
I messed up.

01:16.161 --> 01:17.261
(mocks):
"I messed up."

01:17.295 --> 01:18.562
You went back
to 17th-century Jamestown

01:18.597 --> 01:20.131
and you gave guns
to the Indians.

01:20.165 --> 01:21.298
And now they're
running everything!

01:21.333 --> 01:23.000
I said I messed up,
all right?!

01:23.034 --> 01:24.301
I guess I just have

01:24.336 --> 01:25.703
this pathological need
to be liked.

01:25.737 --> 01:27.071
Well, it's not
a good trait.

01:27.105 --> 01:28.472
Aw, come on.
Don't say that.

01:28.507 --> 01:29.974
Let me buy you lunch.

01:30.008 --> 01:32.309
Thanks to you, the Indians
killed all the white people

01:32.344 --> 01:33.544
and took over America.

01:33.578 --> 01:35.446
I mean, you're the one
who's always saying,

01:35.480 --> 01:36.680
"Never alter the past."

01:36.715 --> 01:38.182
Oh, come on, Brian,
let's be honest.

01:38.216 --> 01:39.583
I frequently alter
the past.

01:39.618 --> 01:43.187
All right, so, I guess
we'll split the bill 13 ways?

01:43.221 --> 01:46.157
Hey, Judas, he ordered,
like, five margaritas.

01:46.191 --> 01:48.759
All you had
was a salad.

01:57.769 --> 01:59.136
You know, it actually
looks like

01:59.171 --> 02:01.172
the Native Americans have
done all right for themselves.

02:01.206 --> 02:03.207
They do seem to have
a lot of shops.

02:09.581 --> 02:12.283
And it looks like they have
medical care as well.

02:12.317 --> 02:14.885
Ma'am, I'm sorry,
but I've done this

02:14.920 --> 02:17.822
several times today, and your
husband just isn't responding.

02:17.856 --> 02:19.557
Well, have you
tried this?

02:19.591 --> 02:21.625
Ma'am, this is a place
of medicine.

02:21.660 --> 02:23.894
And their popular music
is interesting.

02:23.929 --> 02:26.096
And here it is,
the number one song

02:26.131 --> 02:28.566
for the 10,000th week in a row,

02:28.600 --> 02:30.668
"Huya-Huya-Huya"!

02:30.702 --> 02:33.304
(chanting):
♪ Huya-Huya-Huya-Huya

02:33.338 --> 02:36.273
♪ Huya-Huya-Huya-Huya...

02:36.308 --> 02:37.441
Well, no matter..

02:37.476 --> 02:39.210
We better restore things
to the way they were.

02:39.244 --> 02:40.811
Let's go back to Jamestown
and undo...

02:40.846 --> 02:42.580
Oh, dear.
What? What is it?

02:42.614 --> 02:43.681
The return pad.

02:45.217 --> 02:47.485
Looks like it took some
bullets during our escape.

02:47.519 --> 02:49.253
Doesn't seem
to be working.

02:49.287 --> 02:50.254
Hmm.

02:50.288 --> 02:51.589
What?
Wha-What is that?

02:51.623 --> 02:52.723
What is that, "Hmm"?

02:52.757 --> 02:54.191
Wha-What are
you looking for?

02:54.226 --> 02:56.093
What are you looking
for specifically?

02:56.127 --> 02:57.127
Yeah, that's right.

02:57.162 --> 02:58.696
But I'm afraid
our only hope

02:58.730 --> 03:00.130
is that perhaps
the alternate Stewie

03:00.165 --> 03:01.298
in this universe
has created

03:01.333 --> 03:03.067
his own time machine
that we can use.

03:03.101 --> 03:04.168
Well, let's hope so,

03:04.202 --> 03:05.636
because otherwise
they're gonna kill us.

03:05.670 --> 03:06.971
Yeah, or stick us on

03:07.005 --> 03:08.485
some kind of white
people reservation.

03:10.242 --> 03:11.742
Don't give them
any money.

03:11.776 --> 03:14.211
They're just gonna spend
it all on deviled eggs.

03:21.920 --> 03:24.555
Well, there's my brave warrior.

03:24.589 --> 03:25.890
How was work today?

03:25.924 --> 03:27.057
Oh, it was so annoying.

03:27.092 --> 03:28.626
Everyone was coming around
with their daughters,

03:28.660 --> 03:29.827
selling Girl Scout corn.

03:29.861 --> 03:32.696
Made me feel so guilty,
I had to buy, like, 20 ears.

03:32.731 --> 03:36.033
Hey, shot in the dark, but,
uh, is anyone else in the mood

03:36.067 --> 03:40.738
to run really fast
in one direction?

03:40.772 --> 03:42.873
Look, there it is!

03:42.908 --> 03:45.142
And it looks like he has
a return pad, too.

03:45.176 --> 03:46.510
All right, come on.

03:46.545 --> 03:49.613
Let's get back to Jamestown
and set things right.

03:49.648 --> 03:51.649
(zapping, whirring)

03:57.322 --> 03:59.657
Look, there you are
giving them the guns!

04:04.195 --> 04:05.563
What the hell
are you doing there?

04:05.597 --> 04:07.398
You told me you were
checking on the horses.

04:07.432 --> 04:08.553
Well, she's a
sizeable woman.

04:08.567 --> 04:09.567
That's what I meant.

04:15.106 --> 04:16.106
All right, come on.

04:16.141 --> 04:17.421
We've got to get
those guns back.

04:17.442 --> 04:19.076
Hey, hi,
how are you today?

04:19.110 --> 04:20.477
Good to see you.

04:20.512 --> 04:22.012
Listen, uh, we've
just been informed

04:22.047 --> 04:23.480
that these guns
are defective.

04:23.515 --> 04:25.449
Uh, we're from
the gun company.

04:25.483 --> 04:26.884
Um, anyway, you know how

04:26.918 --> 04:29.320
we like to take things back
from you people?

04:29.354 --> 04:31.589
Well, we're gonna go ahead
and take these back.

04:31.623 --> 04:32.990
That's right.
We're from GunCo..

04:33.024 --> 04:34.491
(hushed): Shut up,
you're blowing it.

04:34.526 --> 04:36.460
Where you taking guns?

04:36.494 --> 04:39.163
Brian, grab
the return pad!

04:39.197 --> 04:41.498
Get 'um paleface!

04:43.368 --> 04:46.303
They're gone.
We can talk normal now.

04:50.575 --> 04:52.309
Well, everything looks
like it should.

04:52.344 --> 04:54.244
Only one way
to be certain.

04:54.279 --> 04:56.246
Okay, let's do
a quick search here.

04:56.281 --> 04:57.881
"Native American
role models."

04:57.916 --> 04:58.949
"Zero results found."

04:58.984 --> 05:00.517
Looks like we did it, Brian.

05:00.552 --> 05:01.752
Ah, thank God!

05:01.786 --> 05:03.954
Hey, what are you gonna do
with all those guns?

05:03.989 --> 05:06.390
I don't know, sell 'em
to some messed up kid?

05:09.995 --> 05:11.195
(metallic banging)

05:11.229 --> 05:12.730
What the hell is that?

05:12.764 --> 05:14.164
Stewie?

05:18.703 --> 05:20.270
Oh, my God!
What are you doing?!

05:20.305 --> 05:22.940
This time machine has
almost killed us

05:22.974 --> 05:24.174
a hundred times, Brian.

05:24.209 --> 05:26.110
And yesterday was
just too close a call.

05:26.144 --> 05:28.012
So I've decided
to get rid of it

05:28.046 --> 05:29.647
before something
irreparable happens.

05:29.681 --> 05:31.482
But, Stewie,
your-your time machine,

05:31.516 --> 05:32.850
it's, like, your crowning
achievement.

05:32.884 --> 05:34.485
I-I can't believe
you'd just destroy it.

05:34.519 --> 05:36.053
Trust me, Brian,
it's for the best.

05:36.087 --> 05:38.322
And, hey, at least I've
had some fun with it.

05:38.356 --> 05:40.190
Do you know what
I did last week?

05:40.225 --> 05:41.492
I time-traveled
ahead to Christmas

05:41.526 --> 05:43.360
so I wouldn't have
to wait all year

05:43.395 --> 05:44.962
for the new toys
to come out.

05:44.996 --> 05:46.897
Wow, good timing,
little fella.

05:46.931 --> 05:48.465
This just came out today.

05:48.500 --> 05:50.260
(chuckles) Yeah, I kind
of knew that already.

05:50.268 --> 05:52.036
Would you like to donate
an extra dollar

05:52.070 --> 05:53.270
to the Quahog Food Bank?

05:53.304 --> 05:54.638
Oh, sure.

05:54.673 --> 05:57.107
And would you like to come
home and clean my toilet,

05:57.142 --> 05:58.976
while we're both doing
stuff for each other?

06:10.622 --> 06:12.723
(sighs)
Well, I guess that's it.

06:12.757 --> 06:14.692
This is, like,
the end of an era.

06:14.726 --> 06:16.226
Oh, sack up, you twink.

06:16.261 --> 06:17.594
It's just a machine.

06:17.629 --> 06:19.329
God, look at all
the perfectly good stuff

06:19.364 --> 06:21.065
that people just throw out
for no reason.

06:21.099 --> 06:22.566
I don't know.
It's mostly twin mattresses.

06:22.600 --> 06:23.967
If you have
a twin mattress,

06:24.002 --> 06:26.136
aren't you pretty much
a failure as a human being?

06:26.171 --> 06:27.237
No, I mean it.

06:27.272 --> 06:28.839
Like, here, look at this
street hockey net.

06:28.873 --> 06:30.007
It's in great shape.

06:30.041 --> 06:32.242
You know, we should take this
home and set it up.

06:32.277 --> 06:33.677
Get some fresh air,
get some exercise.

06:33.712 --> 06:35.546
Well, I suppose I could
use a new hobby

06:35.580 --> 06:37.047
now that the time
machine is gone.

06:37.082 --> 06:38.749
You sure you're
not gonna miss that thing?

06:38.783 --> 06:40.384
Nah, I was getting
bored of it anyway.

06:40.418 --> 06:41.618
You know, not every
historical event

06:41.653 --> 06:43.320
is all it's
cracked up to be.

06:43.354 --> 06:45.222
Women, we've spent
decades fighting

06:45.256 --> 06:46.757
for our right to vote.

06:46.791 --> 06:50.060
So, when you go to those
booths and cast your ballot,

06:50.095 --> 06:53.831
remember, Warren G. Harding
is way the cutest!

06:53.865 --> 06:55.265
(all squealing, giggling)

06:55.300 --> 07:00.003
♪ Oh, girls just
want to have fun... ♪

07:01.873 --> 07:03.907
Okay, Brian, I'm just
putting this out there,

07:03.942 --> 07:05.075
but I'm a baby,

07:05.110 --> 07:07.411
and only dicks
don't let babies win.

07:07.445 --> 07:08.912
God, look at
this day, huh?

07:08.947 --> 07:10.881
You know, usually, I'd be
sitting inside writing,

07:10.915 --> 07:12.649
you'd be working on one
of your machines.

07:12.684 --> 07:14.384
But here we are
enjoying it.

07:14.419 --> 07:16.353
Yes, it is a nice change
of pace.

07:16.387 --> 07:18.522
Oh, wait, I got to go
grab my knee pads.

07:18.556 --> 07:20.758
I was using them for...
for-for this other thing.

07:20.792 --> 07:22.392
Anyway,
I'll be right back.

07:26.397 --> 07:27.698
Brian, look out!

07:32.070 --> 07:33.771
Ah, Brian!

07:33.805 --> 07:35.672
Oh, my God!

07:36.674 --> 07:39.143
Brian! Brian, can you hear me?!

07:39.177 --> 07:42.279
Holy crap!
What the hell happened?!

07:42.313 --> 07:44.348
That guy sucked.

07:49.689 --> 07:52.324
Mom, is Brian gonna be okay?

07:52.359 --> 07:53.659
I don't know, Meg.

07:53.693 --> 07:55.994
But this is the best
veterinarian in Quahog.

07:56.029 --> 07:58.163
Brian's getting
the finest care there is.

07:58.198 --> 08:00.499
He had to share the ambulance
with a chicken.

08:00.533 --> 08:03.602
Um, aren't you supposed
to be running around?

08:03.636 --> 08:04.970
Don't talk to me.

08:05.004 --> 08:07.706
You have a bad reputation
in the chicken community.

08:07.741 --> 08:09.241
Doctor, how is Brian?

08:09.275 --> 08:10.709
Is he gonna be okay?

08:10.744 --> 08:13.479
I'm so sorry, Mrs. Griffin,
but Brian's injuries

08:13.513 --> 08:15.814
are just too severe
for us to save him.

08:15.849 --> 08:17.883
I don't know
how much longer he has,

08:17.917 --> 08:20.686
but I suggest that you all go in
and say your good-byes.

08:31.331 --> 08:33.332
(monitor beeping steadily)

08:36.336 --> 08:38.070
Oh, my God!

08:38.104 --> 08:39.271
Brian, no!

08:39.305 --> 08:40.773
I can't believe it.

08:40.807 --> 08:42.474
Y-You're really...

08:42.509 --> 08:44.276
Damn it, Brian,
you can't die!

08:44.310 --> 08:46.378
We were gonna do
so many things together.

08:46.413 --> 08:47.646
We were gonna become
windsurfers.

08:47.680 --> 08:49.882
I was gonna be
a little better than you,

08:49.916 --> 08:51.383
but we were both
gonna be good!

08:51.418 --> 08:52.885
(groans softly)

08:52.919 --> 08:54.620
You guys,
I think, I think

08:54.654 --> 08:56.388
Brian's trying
to say something!

08:56.423 --> 09:01.427
You... you've given me
a wonderful life.

09:01.461 --> 09:03.328
I love you all.

09:06.866 --> 09:08.400
(monitor flatlines)

09:10.670 --> 09:13.405
I'm sorry, he's gone.

09:13.440 --> 09:15.541
Oh, my God, he's...

09:15.575 --> 09:18.944
(voice breaking):
Yes, Chris, I'm afraid...

09:18.978 --> 09:21.914
I'm afraid that
our Brian is dead!

09:50.844 --> 09:53.812
Guys, I'm gonna need a few
minutes alone upstairs.

09:53.847 --> 09:57.382
I got to do, like,
a-a sad yank.

09:57.417 --> 09:59.451
Stupid street hockey.

09:59.486 --> 10:01.553
I wish we never went
to that dump.

10:01.588 --> 10:03.088
Oh, this is all my fault.

10:03.122 --> 10:05.924
I'm the one who had to throw
away the damn time machine.

10:05.959 --> 10:07.326
Wait a minute. That's it!

10:07.360 --> 10:09.495
I've just got to rebuild
the time machine,

10:09.529 --> 10:11.663
and then I can go back
and save Brian!

10:12.966 --> 10:14.867
(tires screech)

10:14.901 --> 10:17.636
Oh, happy birthday, Neil!

10:20.740 --> 10:22.508
Stewie! How are you?

10:22.542 --> 10:25.210
Oh, not well, Yusuf.
I need your help.

10:25.245 --> 10:27.379
I have to track down
a titanium capacitor

10:27.413 --> 10:29.681
for a high-wattage mainframe
I'm going to construct.

10:29.716 --> 10:30.816
Titanium capacitors?

10:30.850 --> 10:32.918
Sorry, there's no way
to get those anymore.

10:32.952 --> 10:34.052
What do you mean?

10:34.087 --> 10:35.754
You've gotten them
for me before.

10:35.788 --> 10:39.324
I know, but the one guy who made
those drew a doodle of Mohammed,

10:39.359 --> 10:41.193
so he's not around anymore.

10:41.227 --> 10:43.128
He wasn't even doing it
on purpose,

10:43.162 --> 10:45.264
just absentmindedly
while talking on the phone.

10:45.298 --> 10:47.332
But it was Mohammed, so...

10:47.367 --> 10:48.534
you know.

10:48.568 --> 10:49.535
Oh, that's awful.

10:49.569 --> 10:52.004
Okay, Yusuf,
thanks, anyway.

10:52.038 --> 10:53.839
Oh, dear, Rupert,
without those capacitors,

10:53.873 --> 10:55.607
it'll be completely impossible

10:55.642 --> 10:57.309
to build
a functioning time machine.

10:57.343 --> 10:58.443
(sighs)

10:58.478 --> 11:02.180
I guess that means Brian
is really gone for good.

11:05.218 --> 11:07.219
(sobbing)

11:09.789 --> 11:11.657
Thank you so much
for coming, guys.

11:11.691 --> 11:13.792
Brian would be very
thankful that you're here.

11:13.826 --> 11:14.960
No problem, Lois.

11:14.994 --> 11:16.595
You know, I usually
don't come to funerals.

11:16.629 --> 11:17.930
Since I'm a cop,

11:17.964 --> 11:20.632
I always wind up getting hit
by a black woman with a purse.

11:20.667 --> 11:22.501
You find the man
who did this.

11:22.535 --> 11:24.703
Uh, ma'am, what's your relationship to Brian?
You bring him to justice!

11:24.737 --> 11:26.471
You make sure this angel child
didn't die for nothing!

11:26.506 --> 11:28.240
Are you sure you're at
the right funeral?

11:28.274 --> 11:29.624
Ma'am, Brian's a dog.

11:29.649 --> 11:30.976
You show the world
why you became a cop!

11:30.977 --> 11:32.544
Okay.

11:34.581 --> 11:37.316
Thank you, everyone,
for coming today.

11:37.350 --> 11:39.952
None of you people came
to my funeral!

11:42.455 --> 11:45.958
I know Peter wanted
to say a few words.

11:45.992 --> 11:47.159
(sighs))

11:47.193 --> 11:49.461
Oh, boy, this is, um...

11:49.495 --> 11:52.664
this is probably the worst pain
I've ever had to go through,

11:52.699 --> 11:55.334
and I've sprained
my ankle twice.

11:55.368 --> 11:57.736
Um... the truth is,

11:57.770 --> 11:59.504
Brian wasn't just my dog.

11:59.539 --> 12:02.207
He was my best friend
in the whole world.

12:02.241 --> 12:04.876
He was smart, he was loyal,

12:04.911 --> 12:08.046
and I guess what
I'm trying to say is

12:08.081 --> 12:10.315
he was like a brother to me.

12:10.350 --> 12:12.551
(voice breaking):
And I-I'm gonna spend

12:12.585 --> 12:14.720
the rest of my life missing him.

12:14.754 --> 12:16.355
(sniffling)

12:16.389 --> 12:18.323
We're all gonna
miss him, Peter.

12:18.358 --> 12:21.193
We're all gonna miss him
very much.

12:21.227 --> 12:23.228
(crying)

12:24.230 --> 12:25.430
Damn it, Ortiz.

12:25.465 --> 12:27.466
Stop swinging at junk.

12:56.129 --> 12:58.563
My God, I almost served
Brian again.

12:58.598 --> 13:01.199
I can't believe he's been gone
a whole month.

13:01.234 --> 13:03.068
Can we at least get rid
of the doggie door

13:03.102 --> 13:04.870
so Chris doesn't keep
getting stuck in it?

13:04.904 --> 13:07.639
Every day I get
a little further in.

13:07.674 --> 13:09.207
You know, I hate to say it,

13:09.242 --> 13:10.942
but I-I'm starting to think
the only way

13:10.977 --> 13:13.845
to stop missing Brian
is to get a new dog.

13:13.880 --> 13:16.615
Or do we all get
Ghostbuster jumpsuits

13:16.649 --> 13:17.816
with our names on them?

13:17.850 --> 13:19.451
♪ Who you gonna call?

13:19.485 --> 13:21.353
♪ The Griffins.

13:21.387 --> 13:22.688
I'm serious, Peter.

13:22.722 --> 13:24.956
I know it might take time
to find the right dog,

13:24.991 --> 13:26.892
but I really feel like
it might be

13:26.926 --> 13:28.727
the best way for us
to move on.

13:28.761 --> 13:30.195
Yeah, Dad.
That would be nice.

13:30.229 --> 13:32.798
All right, well, I guess
it couldn't hurt to look.

13:32.832 --> 13:34.266
Really? Another dog?

13:34.300 --> 13:36.168
Look, I miss Brian
more than any of you,

13:36.202 --> 13:37.869
but at least now
no one's forcing us

13:37.904 --> 13:40.405
to watch
those awful PBS documentaries.

13:40.440 --> 13:44.142
<i>ANNOUNCER: And now PBS presents
Albino Children Are Normal.</i>

13:44.177 --> 13:47.612
So, with the exception of not
being able to go out in the sun,

13:47.647 --> 13:48.914
you're completely normal?

13:48.948 --> 13:50.949
Yes. The moon is my sun.

13:50.983 --> 13:52.617
I like to kill beetles.

13:52.652 --> 13:54.419
Beetles are teachers.

13:54.454 --> 13:56.855
I sleep with a fork.

13:59.892 --> 14:02.427
(birds singing, dogs barking)

14:02.462 --> 14:05.363
All right, guys, time to
find a new family dog.

14:05.398 --> 14:06.965
Hey, Chris,
you're kind of a creep.

14:06.999 --> 14:08.133
You should get a ferret.

14:08.167 --> 14:09.868
You're right, I am.

14:09.902 --> 14:11.203
And I will!

14:11.237 --> 14:12.571
(dogs barking)

14:12.605 --> 14:15.240
I don't know how to choose
between all these dogs.

14:15.274 --> 14:16.708
(New Jersey accent):
Hey, you, green pants.

14:16.743 --> 14:18.944
I hear you're looking for a dog
or something.

14:18.978 --> 14:21.446
Oh, finally, someone who sounds
like they went to college.

14:21.481 --> 14:23.081
Hi, I'm Peter.
Vinny.

14:23.116 --> 14:24.683
Oh, Vinny, like the Pooh.

14:24.717 --> 14:26.084
Hey, that's kind of cool.

14:26.119 --> 14:27.185
So, what's your story?

14:27.220 --> 14:28.854
Well, you know,
I'm just a regular guy.

14:28.888 --> 14:30.722
You throw a stick,
maybe I bring it back.

14:30.757 --> 14:33.792
Maybe I bring back
an iPhone that fell off a truck.

14:33.826 --> 14:34.960
So, what do you say?

14:34.994 --> 14:36.328
You want me as your dog?

14:36.362 --> 14:39.364
Yeah, I think I do!

14:39.398 --> 14:41.399
Hey, just out of curiosity,
what breed are you?

14:41.434 --> 14:43.235
Actually, I'm a pussy hound.

14:43.269 --> 14:44.703
Really? All right!

14:44.737 --> 14:47.038
That just means
I'm one-sixteenth cat.

14:47.073 --> 14:48.840
Oh, I wish you
hadn't told me that.

14:55.239 --> 14:57.607
Well, Vinny,
welcome to our home.

14:57.641 --> 14:59.042
All right,
this is good.

14:59.076 --> 15:00.710
This is good.

15:00.744 --> 15:02.612
Hey, you guys got a good
banging-broads couch,

15:02.646 --> 15:03.680
you know that?

15:03.714 --> 15:05.148
Vinny,
you must be starving.

15:05.182 --> 15:06.683
What can I make you
for dinner?

15:06.717 --> 15:07.717
Are you kidding?

15:07.751 --> 15:08.985
You're a hardworking lady.

15:09.019 --> 15:10.720
So, tonight, I'm gonna
make you dinner.

15:10.754 --> 15:12.088
Wow.
(chuckles)

15:12.122 --> 15:13.756
Well, thank you, Vinny.

15:13.791 --> 15:14.924
Whoa, whoa, hang on, Lois.

15:14.959 --> 15:16.392
You promised me
we were all eating

15:16.427 --> 15:17.760
my Play-Doh pasta tonight.

15:17.795 --> 15:20.263
Oh, Stewie, did you
make something?

15:20.297 --> 15:22.832
Mmm, oh, it's so yummy.

15:22.866 --> 15:24.834
Num, num, num, num, num.

15:24.868 --> 15:27.637
You better mean
those "nums."

15:27.671 --> 15:29.205
Hey, you,
what's your name?

15:29.239 --> 15:30.373
Chris.

15:30.407 --> 15:32.108
How about I call
you Hat Boy?

15:32.142 --> 15:33.610
Hell yeah!

15:33.644 --> 15:34.844
Lower your voice, Chris.

15:34.878 --> 15:36.646
It's Hat Boy, bitch!

15:36.680 --> 15:37.947
Wow, look at you, Vinny.

15:37.982 --> 15:39.549
It's like you're already
part of the family.

15:39.583 --> 15:41.951
I don't know, fat man,
I think you're jumping the gun.

15:41.986 --> 15:44.454
I mean, crucifixion seemed great
at first, too.

15:44.488 --> 15:46.189
Why are you
doing that?

15:46.223 --> 15:47.890
'Cause I'm mad at this guy.

15:47.925 --> 15:50.026
I'm mad at this jar of
pickles I can't open.

15:50.060 --> 15:51.194
Nail it up.

15:54.898 --> 15:57.266
Hey, guys, I want you to
meet our new dog, Vinny.

15:57.301 --> 15:59.035
Vinny, this is Joe
and Quagmire.

15:59.069 --> 16:00.336
Hey, Vinny.
How you doing?

16:00.371 --> 16:01.371
Real good.

16:01.405 --> 16:02.839
Hey, so I hear
you're a cop.

16:02.873 --> 16:03.973
That's right.

16:04.008 --> 16:05.008
Hey, that's great.

16:05.042 --> 16:06.342
Maybe sometime
you could take me

16:06.377 --> 16:08.478
on a ride-along with you,
show me the neighborhood.

16:08.512 --> 16:10.913
Yeah, I don't take people out
on ride-alongs anymore.

16:10.948 --> 16:13.950
I had a bad experience
with Grimace.

16:16.487 --> 16:20.323
I got to be honest, I can't
get a bead on you, man.

16:29.366 --> 16:31.768
Oh, boy, Vinny, hanging out
with you has been the best.

16:31.802 --> 16:32.835
You want another beer?

16:32.870 --> 16:34.103
No.

16:34.138 --> 16:35.304
I'd love one!

16:35.339 --> 16:36.606
(both laugh)

16:36.640 --> 16:38.408
What? What? The first
thing you said was...

16:38.442 --> 16:40.209
(laughing hysterically):
Oh, my God!

16:40.244 --> 16:42.512
You're on... you're, like,
on another level, Vinny!

16:43.847 --> 16:44.914
Congratulations.

16:44.948 --> 16:46.649
You've won over
a complete imbecile.

16:46.684 --> 16:47.784
Whoa, whoa, whoa!

16:47.818 --> 16:49.485
Did you just speak
ill of the man

16:49.520 --> 16:51.154
who busts his tail
providing for you?

16:51.188 --> 16:53.289
He spent my college fund
on puppet clothes.

16:53.323 --> 16:55.658
Hey, don't get fresh
with me, Estudio.

16:55.693 --> 16:56.893
That's not my name.

16:56.927 --> 16:58.995
That's not even
an Italian version of my name.

16:59.029 --> 17:00.263
Oh, whatever.

17:00.297 --> 17:02.131
PETER: Hey, Vinny, you
want to see a puppet show?

17:02.166 --> 17:04.434
They all got
leather jackets!

17:04.468 --> 17:05.702
God, what a jerk.

17:05.736 --> 17:07.236
If this guy thinks
he's sticking around,

17:07.271 --> 17:08.504
he's sorely mistaken.

17:08.539 --> 17:09.939
I'm going to ruin him.

17:09.973 --> 17:12.408
Just like I ruined
that magician's act.

17:14.111 --> 17:15.244
(applause)

17:15.279 --> 17:17.280
People, people, people.

17:17.314 --> 17:19.649
This man drives a Saturn.

17:22.920 --> 17:24.787
All right, Rupert,
I've been left with no choice.

17:24.822 --> 17:27.156
I'm going to tell Vinny
so much bad news

17:27.191 --> 17:28.424
about Italian-Americans

17:28.459 --> 17:30.426
that he goes
into cardiac arrest.

17:30.461 --> 17:31.894
Vinny, there you are.

17:31.929 --> 17:33.262
I have some
terrible news.

17:33.297 --> 17:36.099
Sal Annuncio's kid was
killed by a falling piano!

17:36.133 --> 17:38.234
Oh! How could this happen?!

17:38.268 --> 17:39.869
That kid was
an honors student!

17:39.903 --> 17:41.871
He'd just applied
to SUNY Albany.

17:41.905 --> 17:43.573
<i>He just got in
to SUNY Albany.</i>

17:43.607 --> 17:45.007
(groans):
Oh!

17:45.042 --> 17:46.275
This is too much, Stewie.

17:46.310 --> 17:47.443
I can't take it.

17:47.478 --> 17:49.011
Oh, my God, it's working.

17:49.046 --> 17:50.813
Hey, Vinny, Bobby
Lammaduro's kid

17:50.848 --> 17:52.348
just got out of
the hospital.

17:52.382 --> 17:54.117
He beat the cancer!

17:54.151 --> 17:55.818
Oh, thank heavens.

17:55.853 --> 17:59.088
Big man in the sky
knows what he's doing.

17:59.123 --> 18:00.456
Chris, what are you doing?

18:00.491 --> 18:02.759
Call me a creep,
you piece of crap!

18:06.263 --> 18:07.897
Hey, Doonesbury,

18:07.931 --> 18:10.733
how about a joke that ain't
for fancy millionaires?

18:10.768 --> 18:12.769
(Stewie sniffling)

18:14.938 --> 18:17.974
(sobbing)

18:18.008 --> 18:20.009
(sobbing continues)

18:21.411 --> 18:23.713
Stewie, what's with
the waterworks?

18:23.747 --> 18:25.948
Oh, um, um...
I-I just...

18:25.983 --> 18:28.050
I realized I paid too much
for my muffler.

18:28.085 --> 18:29.752
Come on, kid,
quit crying.

18:29.787 --> 18:31.521
You're gonna get
your pants wrinkled.

18:31.555 --> 18:32.889
What's the matter?

18:32.923 --> 18:34.090
(sighs)
Fine.

18:34.124 --> 18:35.725
Do you want to know
what's the matter?

18:35.759 --> 18:37.026
You're the matter.

18:37.060 --> 18:39.262
Everyone in this family
is so damn thrilled with you,

18:39.296 --> 18:40.963
they've forgotten
all about Brian.

18:40.998 --> 18:42.365
Well, I'm not thrilled.

18:42.399 --> 18:44.100
I'll never forget Brian.

18:44.134 --> 18:45.802
He was my best friend.

18:45.836 --> 18:49.071
You're not supposed to lose
your best friend at my age.

18:49.106 --> 18:51.541
You're not supposed
to lose him ever!

18:51.575 --> 18:54.644
Look, Stewie, I know it
doesn't seem like it now,

18:54.678 --> 18:56.112
but you're gonna be okay.

18:56.146 --> 18:57.980
Yeah, right.
How do you know?

18:58.015 --> 19:02.151
Because, I once had a thing
happen to me with some stuff.

19:02.186 --> 19:03.719
Really?
That's what's happening to me.

19:03.754 --> 19:04.987
Stuff.

19:05.022 --> 19:06.956
You know, before you guys
found me at the shop,

19:06.990 --> 19:08.691
I lived with an old man
named Leo.

19:08.725 --> 19:11.127
It was just him and me
in a tiny apartment,

19:11.161 --> 19:12.495
and we got pretty tight.

19:12.529 --> 19:14.397
He kind of became
my whole world.

19:14.431 --> 19:18.067
By the time he passed away,
it was real tough.

19:18.101 --> 19:20.069
So, how did he die?

19:20.103 --> 19:21.737
He tried to go
to a yoga class,

19:21.772 --> 19:24.373
and on the first pose, his
ball sack split in half.

19:24.408 --> 19:25.441
Really?

19:25.475 --> 19:26.742
<i>Yeah, it was
on Dateline.</i>

19:26.777 --> 19:28.044
It was a whole thing.

19:28.078 --> 19:31.280
Anyway, I guess Leo was
kind of like my Brian.

19:31.315 --> 19:33.950
Well, so,
how'd you get over him?

19:33.984 --> 19:35.918
Well, I met your family
in the pet store.

19:35.953 --> 19:37.787
When I saw you guys
looking for a dog,

19:37.821 --> 19:39.689
I kind of sensed
you were all going through

19:39.723 --> 19:41.524
the same feelings of loss
that I was.

19:41.558 --> 19:42.925
And it made me think,

19:42.960 --> 19:44.927
hey, maybe I was meant
to find these guys.

19:44.962 --> 19:47.563
Maybe we were meant
to be together.

19:47.598 --> 19:49.198
You know what, Vinny?

19:49.233 --> 19:51.901
I'm starting to think
that may be true.

19:51.935 --> 19:54.470
(chuckles)
Bring it in, Stewie.

19:58.976 --> 20:00.977
Hey, Stewie,
now that we're pals,

20:01.011 --> 20:02.778
maybe you can
answer me something.

20:02.813 --> 20:05.114
Why does your living room
smell so much like puke?

20:05.148 --> 20:07.183
Somebody throw up a
lot in there once?

20:07.217 --> 20:10.253
Vinny, my friend, you got a lot
of stories to catch up on.

20:10.287 --> 20:11.787
I got most of them on DVD.

20:11.822 --> 20:12.889
Oh, cool.

20:12.923 --> 20:14.690
Like, by season
and stuff?

20:14.725 --> 20:17.460
(chuckles)
No, not by season.

20:21.965 --> 20:24.133
Boy, I can't remember
the last time

20:24.167 --> 20:25.935
Stewie fell asleep
this early.

20:25.969 --> 20:28.504
Well, I think the little guy
had a long day.

20:28.538 --> 20:30.907
All right, come on,
time for bed, Vinny.

20:30.941 --> 20:33.209
I'll tell Peter to turn off
the nightlight this time.

20:33.243 --> 20:35.878
PETER: No! I need it
for in case there's witches!

20:35.913 --> 20:37.146
You know, actually, Lois,

20:37.180 --> 20:39.582
I think I'm gonna sleep
in here with Stewie tonight.

20:39.616 --> 20:41.617
Oh, well,
isn't that nice.

20:41.652 --> 20:43.319
Sleep tight, you two.

20:46.423 --> 20:47.523
(yawns)

20:47.557 --> 20:48.591
Good night, Vinny.

20:48.625 --> 20:50.126
Good night, Stewie.

20:50.374 --> 20:52.371
Hey, you guys know
which room is Peter's?

20:52.372 --> 20:53.620
Across the hall.

20:53.645 --> 20:56.536
Ah, it's too bright in there.
Never mind.

20:56.537 --> 21:57.537
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru